{ The eigenvalue system reads $Q \mathbf{x} = \lambda \mathbf{x}$. Adding
a vertex $n+1$ of value $0$ connected to all other vertices we obtain 
the following identity.
\begin{equation}\label{esQ}
\begin{pmatrix} Q+ I_n & \mathbf{1} \\ \mathbf{1}^T & n \end{pmatrix}  
\begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{x} \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} (\lambda+1) \mathbf{x} \\ \mathbf{1}^T \mathbf{x} \end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $I_n$ is the $n \times n$ identity matrix and $\mathbf{1}$ is
the vector of ones of dimension $n$.
	Then, if $\mathbf{1}^T \mathbf{x}= \sum_i x_i = 0$ the new eigenvalue is $\lambda' = \lambda +1$. }

\label{addA}
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph affording an eigenvalue $\lambda \neq 0$ for $A$ { associated with} an eigenvector $\mathbf{x}$ and $W \subset V$ such that $\sum_{i\in W} x_i = 0$.  Then the new graph $G'$ obtained by adding a node connected to all the nodes of $W$ has the same eigenvalue $\lambda$
for the eigenvector $\mathbf{x}^{\prime}$ obtained by extending $\mathbf{x}$ by a zero component.

The eigenvalue system reads $A \mathbf{x} = \lambda \mathbf{x}$. Adding
a vertex $n+1$ of value $0$ connected to all the vertices in $W$ we obtain
the following identity.
\begin{equation}\label{esA}
\begin{pmatrix} A & \mathbf{C}_{W} \\ \mathbf{C}_{W}^T & 0 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{x} \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} \lambda \mathbf{x} \\ \mathbf{C}_{W}^T \mathbf{x} \end{pmatrix}
= \lambda \begin{pmatrix}  \mathbf{x} \\ 0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{C}_{W}$ is the vector of dimension $n$ with entries $1$ for vertices
in $W$ and $0$ otherwise.
This shows the result.

\label{xaql}
If $\mathbf{x}$ is an eigenvector of $A, L$ and $Q$ affording eigenvalues $\lambda_A,
\lambda_L, \lambda_Q$, then the graph G is such that { every vertex corresponding to a
non zero component of $\mathbf{x}$ has degree $ d=\lambda_Q-\lambda_A = \lambda_L+\lambda_A = (\lambda_L+\lambda_Q)/2$.}

Take an eigenvector $\textbf{x}$ of $A,$ $L$ and $Q.$ For each vertex $i$, we have 
\begin{eqnarray}
d_{i} x_{i} + \sum_{j \sim i} x_{j} &=& \lambda_{Q} \, x_{i} \label{eq1} \\
d_{i} x_{i} - \sum_{j \sim i} x_{j} &=& \lambda_{L} \, x_{i}. \label{eq2} 
\end{eqnarray}
From \eqref{eq1} - \eqref{eq2}, we get 
\begin{eqnarray}
    \lambda_A = \frac{\lambda_Q -\lambda_{L}}{2}, \label{eq3} \\
2\, d_i \, x_i= (\lambda_Q + \lambda_{L})x_i . ~\label{eq4}
\end{eqnarray}

Equation \eqref{eq4} is satisfied by $x_{i}=0$ or $2d_i = (\lambda_Q + \lambda_{L})$. 
We cannot have $x_i=0$ for all $i$ because $\mathbf{x}$ is a nonzero vector.

\label{thm:JA} 
The $L$-bivalent graphs are the regular bipartite graphs and their extensions obtained by adding edges
between nodes having the same value for a bivalent eigenvector.

(\cite{CRS07})  \label{lem:Qbip}
The least eigenvalue of the signless Laplacian of a connected graph is equal to
0 if and only if the graph is bipartite. In this case 0 is a simple eigenvalue.

\label{thmAL21}(\cite{AL21})
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph on $n$ vertices, and let $S \subset V.$ Then, there is a $\{-1,1\}$-eigenvector associated with eigenvalue $\lambda_A$ for $A$ if and only if 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bivAthm1}
d_{i}(S)-d_{i}(S, \overline{S}) =\lambda_A \mbox{ for all { vertices} } i \in S,
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bivAthm2}
d_{i}(\overline{S})-d_{i}(\overline{S}, S) =\lambda_A \;\; \mbox{  for all { vertices } } i \in \overline{S}.
\end{equation}

\label{thm35}
Assume $\mathbf{x}$ is a common bivalent eigenvector for $A, L$ and $Q$. Then the graph is regular.
It is formed from a regular bipartite graph with any number of additional edges within the two sets of the bipartite graph such that the additional edges induce a regular graph.

\label{lemma:1}
Let $T \;\; (\ne P_n$) be an $A$-bivalent tree on  $n$ vertices. The following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item [(a) ] All leaves, incident to the same vertex, should be in the same set.
    \item [(b) ] There are no two adjacent vertices, say $u$ and $v$ of degrees 2 and 1, respectively, in the same set.
    \item [(c) ] There is no vertex $w \in S$ such that $d_{w}(S) \geq 2$ and $d_{w}(S,\overline{S}) = 0.$ The same holds if $w \in \overline{S}.$
\end{itemize}
